Hey, I ran into an issue with custom providers.
I have custom providers defined in `~/.soulforge/config.json`, and they work in the CLI:
- `soulforge --list-providers` shows them
- `soulforge --list-models <provider>` shows their models
- headless runs with `--model provider/model` also work
But in the TUI, pressing `Ctrl+L` does not show those custom providers in the model picker. Only the built-in providers appear.
So it looks like the custom providers are being registered for the runtime, but the TUI model picker is using a stale provider snapshot that was created before custom providers were loaded.
Quick fix:
when `registerCustomProviders()` runs, also refresh `PROVIDER_CONFIGS` in place so anything holding a reference to it sees the updated list.
Something like:
```js
function registerCustomProviders(configs) {
  const builtinIds = new Set(BUILTIN_PROVIDERS.map((p) => p.id));
  const seen = new Map();
  for (const c of configs) {
    const def = buildCustomProvider(c);
    if (builtinIds.has(c.id) || seen.has(c.id)) {
      def.id = `${c.id}-custom`;
      let id = def.id;
      let n = 2;
      while (builtinIds.has(id) || seen.has(id)) {
        id = `${c.id}-custom-${String(n++)}`;
      }
      def.id = id;
    }
    seen.set(def.id, def);
  }
  allProviders = [...BUILTIN_PROVIDERS, ...seen.values()];
  providerMap = new Map(allProviders.map((p) => [p.id, p]));
  PROVIDER_CONFIGS.splice(
    0,
    PROVIDER_CONFIGS.length,
    ...getAllProviders().map((p) => ({
      id: p.id,
      name: p.name,
      envVar: p.envVar,
      grouped: p.grouped,
      fallbackModels: p.fallbackModels
    }))
  );
}

[opencode.ai](https://opencode.ai/) provides an OpenAI-compatible API gateway with access to models like `kimi-k2.5`, `minimax-m2.7`, and `glm-5.1`. It would be great to have it as a built-in provider similar to OpenRouter.

In the meantime, I've been using it as a custom provider:

```json
{
  "id": "opencode",
  "name": "OpenCode Go",
  "baseURL": "https://opencode.ai/zen/go/v1",
  "envVar": "OPENCODE_API_KEY",
  "models": ["kimi-k2.5", "minimax-m2.7", "glm-5.1"]
}
```

The issue is that during multi-turn conversations with tool calls, requests intermittently fail with a `400` error. Raw response from the provider:

```json
{
  "error": {
    "message": "Provider returned error",
    "code": 400,
    "metadata": {
      "raw": "{\"error\": {\"message\": \"thinking is enabled but reasoning_content is missing in assistant tool call message at index 2\", \"type\": \"invalid_request_error\"}}",
      "provider_name": "Moonshot AI",
      "is_byok": true
    }
  }
}
```

The error is non-deterministic same session works sometimes and fails others, and the index varies across failures. This suggests that when SoulForge reconstructs the conversation history, `reasoning_content` from previous assistant messages (which these models return when thinking mode is active) gets stripped, causing the provider to reject the request.

