## Summary

On macOS, the status-bar memory poller spawns `vmmap --summary` against soulforge's **own PID every 2000 ms**. `vmmap` walks the entire VM map of the process (~260 MB footprint here), stalling it for ~300 ms per call. Result: the TUI hitches/freezes for ~0.3 s on a fixed ~2 s cadence for the whole session.

## Environment

- soulforge: macOS prebuilt binary (latest channel 2.20.8), proxy addon 7.1.54
- OS: macOS 14.8.7, x86_64, kitty terminal
- Default model: `proxy/gemini-3.1-pro-low`

## Root cause

From the bundled JS, the status bar starts a 2 s poll that, on every tick, resolves the main process "footprint" by shelling out to `vmmap`:

```js
function startMemoryPoll(intervalMs = 2000) {   // called with no arg -> 2000 ms
  memPollTimer = setInterval(async () => {
    const mainMB = await getMainFootprintMB();    // <-- every 2 s
    const groups = await collectPidGroups();
    getPerPidRssKB([...]).then(...)               // status bar RSS readout
  }, intervalMs);
}

async function getMainFootprintMB() {
  const rssMB = Math.round(process.memoryUsage().rss / 1024 / 1024);
  const footprint = await macFootprintMB(process.pid);   // unconditional on macOS
  return footprint ?? rssMB;
}

function macFootprintMB(pid) {
  execFile2("vmmap", ["--summary", String(pid)], { timeout: 2000, maxBuffer: 1048576 }, ...)
}
```

`rssMB` (cheap, from `process.memoryUsage().rss`) is already computed, but the function still forks `vmmap` every tick just to read "Physical footprint".

## Evidence (reproduced live)

- `sample <pid> 4` → main thread pegged ~100%, process in `R+` (not idle-waiting).
- `time vmmap --summary <pid>` → **real 0.32 s** per call on a ~260 MB process.
- 6 s watcher on children of the soulforge PID caught 4 `vmmap` spawns, ~every 2 s.

## Impact

Periodic UI freeze (~0.3 s every 2 s), entire session, macOS. Scales with process footprint — worse on large/long sessions.

## Suggested fix

Drop the per-tick `vmmap` fork; use the already-computed RSS:

```js
async function getMainFootprintMB() {
  return Math.round(process.memoryUsage().rss / 1024 / 1024);
}
```

If the precise "Physical footprint" metric is still wanted: sample it rarely (e.g. every 30 s), cache it, and never fork `vmmap` on the 2 s status-bar timer. A toggle/env var to disable the status-bar RSS poll would also help (none currently — `startMemoryPoll()` is called with no argument).

I set `"memory": { "embeddingModel": "openrouter/perplexity/pplx-embed-v1-4b" }` with a working `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`, but `/memory` → Settings always reports `offline (hashbag-v2)`. The routes the memory docs list as verified (e.g. `openrouter/openai/text-embedding-3-small`) behave the same. No error shows up anywhere, which made this fairly painful to track down — here is what I found after reading the source.

`createAiSdkEmbedder` hands the model *string* straight to the AI SDK ([embedder.ts:244](https://github.com/proxysoul/soulforge/blob/8ae117e6045cd2da1f029173343cf602b84427d6/src/core/memory/embedder.ts#L244)):

```ts
const r = await sdkEmbed({ model: modelId, value: text });
```

For string ids the AI SDK resolves through `globalThis.AI_SDK_DEFAULT_PROVIDER ?? gateway`, and nothing in `src/` ever sets `AI_SDK_DEFAULT_PROVIDER` (zero grep hits). So every embedding call goes to the Vercel AI Gateway and wants `AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY` — SoulForge's own provider registry and my OpenRouter key are never consulted.

Chat traffic doesn't have this problem because `resolveModel` builds a `LanguageModel` object through SoulForge's own providers ([provider.ts:117](https://github.com/proxysoul/soulforge/blob/8ae117e6045cd2da1f029173343cf602b84427d6/src/core/llm/provider.ts#L117), [L133](https://github.com/proxysoul/soulforge/blob/8ae117e6045cd2da1f029173343cf602b84427d6/src/core/llm/provider.ts#L133)). Embeddings are the only string-path consumer — presumably because the provider interface only exposes `createModel(): LanguageModel` ([types.ts:46](https://github.com/proxysoul/soulforge/blob/8ae117e6045cd2da1f029173343cf602b84427d6/src/core/llm/providers/types.ts#L46)) and there is simply no embedding constructor to call.

The smoke test then fails and gets swallowed ([manager.ts:545-549](https://github.com/proxysoul/soulforge/blob/8ae117e6045cd2da1f029173343cf602b84427d6/src/core/memory/manager.ts#L545-L549)):

```ts
const probe = await provider.embed("ok");
if (!probe || probe.length === 0) throw new Error("empty embedding");
} catch (_err) {
  this._providerEmbedder = null;
  return null;
}
```

so the UI shows offline with no reason, even though `resolveEmbeddingModel` ([embedder-resolver.ts:72](https://github.com/proxysoul/soulforge/blob/8ae117e6045cd2da1f029173343cf602b84427d6/src/core/memory/embedder-resolver.ts#L72)) already produces good reason strings.

Two follow-on effects worth mentioning:

- The embedder re-wires on every provider switch with that single one-shot probe ([context/manager.ts:1785](https://github.com/proxysoul/soulforge/blob/8ae117e6045cd2da1f029173343cf602b84427d6/src/core/context/manager.ts#L1785), [L1838](https://github.com/proxysoul/soulforge/blob/8ae117e6045cd2da1f029173343cf602b84427d6/src/core/context/manager.ts#L1838), [L1851](https://github.com/proxysoul/soulforge/blob/8ae117e6045cd2da1f029173343cf602b84427d6/src/core/context/manager.ts#L1851)), so one transient 429/5xx flips you to offline until the next switch or a restart.
- The docs' verified-models table can't work as written unless the user happens to have a Vercel gateway credential.

Suggested direction: give the provider abstraction an embedding-model constructor and resolve embedding ids the way `resolveModel` does (or register a global provider that delegates to the registry), and surface the probe failure reason in `/memory` → Settings.

I verified the diagnosis by preloading a script that sets `globalThis.AI_SDK_DEFAULT_PROVIDER` to a minimal provider calling OpenRouter's `/embeddings` — semantic recall comes back immediately.

